# Fragments as editor symbols

A fragment name is written as text, but authors use it as something richer. It can
be declared in one chapter, extended in another, referenced from generated code, and
mentioned in explanatory prose. Readers expect to jump between those places. Authors
expect a rename to change the symbol without rewriting unrelated sentences or
Markdown structure.

The editor can provide those expectations only if it knows where fragment names are
meaningful. Searching every pair of angle brackets is not enough. Markdown links,
examples, escaped text, code spans, tables, and malformed fences all affect whether
the same characters are a symbol or ordinary writing.

## One identity, several kinds of occurrence

A fragment family is identified by its exact name. The editor records every semantic
occurrence of that name with a source path and a range. Occurrences have four roles:

- a **definition** begins a family;
- an **append** contributes more text to an existing family;
- a **reference** expands the family inside fragment code;
- a **mention** discusses the family in explanatory Markdown.

This is intentionally smaller than a compiler's symbol table. Identity does not need
a mutable object with hidden host state. The exact name and the captured occurrence
sequence are enough for completion, navigation, references, hover, and rename
planning.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class FragmentFamily {
        exact name
        ordered contributions
    }
    class Definition {
        source range
    }
    class Append {
        source range
    }
    class Reference {
        source range
    }
    class Mention {
        source range
    }
    FragmentFamily "1" --> "1" Definition : begins at
    FragmentFamily "1" --> "0..*" Append : continues at
    FragmentFamily "1" --> "0..*" Reference : used by
    FragmentFamily "1" --> "0..*" Mention : discussed by
```

The lines mean “shares this family identity,” not ownership of source text. Each
occurrence remains in its original document, and an append is still a declaration
location even though it is not the family's base definition. The diagram omits
output expansion because editor symbols describe authored source, not copies in a
generated file.

In the pure symbol layer these records are `FragmentOccurrence` values. Their lines
and columns use raw UTF-16 positions, the same coordinate convention used by the
editor. The complete tag range supports highlighting and navigation; a rename edit
uses only the inner name range so delimiters remain untouched.

## Parse meaning instead of searching spelling

A textual search produces convincing mistakes. It may rename a tag shown as an
example, match a name inside a longer name, edit a link destination, or choose the
wrong repeated occurrence. Literate instead collects symbols from the same Markdown
and fragment grammars used by the project engine.

Named fence headers contribute definitions and appends only when the shared header
parser accepts them. Their bodies contribute references using the shared tag scanner.
Settings bodies, ordinary code fences, malformed declarations, and output filenames
are not searched as prose.

Prose mentions pass through Markdown's inline parser. This preserves distinctions a
reader already sees: escaped openers are literal; link labels can discuss a symbol,
while destinations and titles are not author-facing symbol text; successful inline
code spans may contain mentions; image alternate text is excluded when its source
coordinates cannot be recovered safely. HTML blocks are conservatively excluded
rather than treating attributes as renameable prose.

Tables need special care because a Markdown parser exposes cell content after
splitting and unescaping it. The collector keeps a map back to raw source columns,
then validates each recovered slice with the fragment scanner. This extra work serves
a simple invariant: every editor range must come from syntax at that exact location,
never from searching for a familiar string after parsing.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Text[Captured document text] --> Block[Parse Markdown blocks]
    Block --> Fence{Accepted named fence?}
    Fence -->|Yes| Declarations[Collect definition or append and body references]
    Fence -->|No| Inline[Parse meaningful inline regions]
    Inline --> Mentions[Collect prose mentions with raw ranges]
    Declarations --> Ordered[Order occurrences by document and source position]
    Mentions --> Ordered
    Ordered --> Check[Ranges retain exact authored spelling]
```

The arrows describe narrowing: each stage discards text that has no fragment-symbol
meaning. They do not describe expansion or generation. The important consequence is
that unresolved references are still retained. A missing family is invalid for
generation but remains exactly the symbol an author may want to define with a quick
fix.

## Completion lowers recall without taking over Markdown

When an author has begun a fragment tag but not finished its name, completion offers
the known families in that workspace project. Outside that context it offers
nothing, leaving ordinary Markdown completion and mapped programming-language
completion to their own providers.

Before answering, completion asks the repository for a read-only refresh. Suggestions
therefore include unsaved contributions from open buffers without writing generated
files. In VS Code this behavior is exposed through a `CompletionItemProvider`. Its
trigger is deliberately lexical: an unmatched `<<` before the cursor asks for
fragment-family names from a coherent project snapshot. Navigation and refactoring
apply stricter occurrence checks before acting.

## Definition, references, and hover answer different questions

“Go to definition” chooses the base definition of the family at the cursor. It does
not choose the nearest append or the place from which generated text happened to
arrive. “Find references” returns uses in fragment bodies and explicit prose mentions,
with declarations included only when the caller asks for them. These policies share
identity and ranges while preserving the different questions users asked.

Hover provides a compact inspection of the family's unexpanded authored code. It is
useful for following an indirection without leaving the explanation, but it is not a
claim about a particular generated occurrence. A shared family may be expanded in
several roots or at several indentation levels; the projection chapters address
services that need those assembled contexts.

VS Code names the adapters `DefinitionProvider`, `ReferenceProvider`, and
`HoverProvider`. Each adapter refreshes read-only state, honors cancellation, locates
the exact occurrence at the cursor, and translates pure source ranges into editor
locations or display content. The language rules stay outside those classes.

## Rename is a project-wide proof before it is an edit

Rename is the most demanding symbol operation because a plausible but incomplete
result can silently break the program or its explanation. Literate plans the complete
change in memory before returning any edit to the editor.

Both names must be valid fragment identities. The old family must have exactly one
base definition. The new name must not collide with any known occurrence, including
an unresolved reference or prose mention that could otherwise be captured by the
rename. Root status cannot be added or removed by changing the name.

Every occurrence of the old name then receives a name-only replacement in a copied
snapshot. The symbol collector parses that preview again. The full ordered occurrence
sequence must survive: paths, lines, roles, and unrelated names remain equal, while
only the requested name changes. If punctuation in the new name would alter a table,
fence, inline-code span, or other Markdown structure, the plan is rejected.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Request[Requested old and new names] --> Names[Validate both identities]
    Names --> Base[Require one unambiguous base definition]
    Base --> Collision[Reject collisions and root-status changes]
    Collision --> Plan[Plan name-only edits for every occurrence]
    Plan --> Copy[Apply edits to copied source text]
    Copy --> Parse[Collect every occurrence again]
    Parse --> Equal{Same ordered syntax except for the new name?}
    Equal -->|No| Reject[Return no edits]
    Equal -->|Yes| Offer[Offer one atomic workspace edit]
```

The arrows form a fail-closed pipeline. No source changes occur along it. Only the
last step hands a proposal to VS Code, which owns applying the `WorkspaceEdit` as one
editor operation. A later document-change event follows the normal project-processing
path. Cancellation likewise returns no partial set.

This validation does not promise that an arbitrary new name is desirable prose. It
proves the narrower property an editor refactoring can establish mechanically: the
recognized symbol structure remains intact.

## Fixes start from current evidence

A missing-reference diagnostic can support a useful quick fix: create an empty family
near the fragment that contains the reference. The human benefit is obvious, but the
diagnostic may already be stale by the time the light bulb appears.

The fix therefore filters by a stable diagnostic code, refreshes the project without
writing, and verifies that the requested buffer still has the captured version and
text. It then requires an exact unresolved reference at the diagnostic range and a
closed, top-level containing fence. Unsafe or ambiguous insertion sites produce no
action.

The result is another proposed `WorkspaceEdit`, attached to the document that owns
the diagnostic rather than whichever editor happens to be active. This is the role
of a VS Code `CodeActionProvider`: translate current semantic evidence into an edit
the host may present and apply, not execute a hidden generation command.

## Structural commands are deliberately separate

Creating a family for the tag at the cursor and splitting a contribution into an
append are direct authoring commands. They use the shared header parser and editor
token locations to preserve the current language and family name. Splitting closes
the current fence and opens an append below the selected line, allowing the
explanation to continue between two contributions to one family.

These commands operate on the active document when explicitly invoked. They are not
rename-like semantic proofs across the whole project, and they should not be confused
with diagnostic quick fixes, which validate a particular current snapshot before
offering an edit. Keeping the distinction visible leaves room for future structural
commands to adopt stronger preview-and-reparse checks without changing symbol
identity.

## Exploration is navigation at a larger scale

Completion and hover help at one cursor position. Definition and references connect
a small set of locations. When an author wants to understand a whole dependency
neighborhood, the same family identities become nodes and their references become
edges. That larger view should not invent a competing symbol model.

The editor tree and the inline preview explorer both consume accepted families and
references, but they present different journeys. Their treatment of repeated edges,
cycles, lazy expansion, and browser resource limits is the subject of the fragment
graph exploration chapter. Before that, we need to understand the preview in which
explanation, semantic links, code, diagrams, and exploration controls meet.
